-- emit/app: derivation is EAGER and elimination is what makes it shippable
-- (`docs/design/backend.md` §9; `static-dispatch-spike.md` §8.5, §9.4).
--
-- Three nominal types, and the compiler derives an `eq` and a `compare` for
-- every one of them whether or not anything calls them — six functions and
-- three `$$order` tables before this pass runs. What the golden shows is
-- which of the nine a `main`-rooted build keeps:
--
--   - `Kept` is compared with `==`, so `Kept$$eq` is emitted. Its payload is
--     a `Payload`, so the derived body names `Payload$$eq` as one of its
--     structural positions — **the transitive edge**, and the one that no
--     `Bir.refs` row and no source line spells. It comes from the `parts`
--     range of the `derived` row, which is §9's third leg read out of a
--     `Derived` row rather than out of a declaration.
--   - `Kept$$compare` and `Payload$$compare` are gone: nothing orders
--     either type. So are the two `$$order` tables that would have gone
--     with those `compare`s — a table is not a node of the graph, it lives
--     and dies with the `compare` whose row it hangs off.
--   - `Unused` is compared nowhere at all, so both of its methods and its
--     `$$order` table are gone entire.
--
-- `Kept` has two constructors, so a surviving `eq` still needs no `$$order`
-- table: only `compare` reads one (§9.4).
import Node exposing (Program)
import String


pub type Payload
    = Payload Int


pub type Kept
    = Empty
    | Holds Payload


pub type Unused
    = Red
    | Green
    | Blue


pub same : Kept, Kept -> String
same a b =
    if a == b then
        "yes"
    else
        "no"


main : Program
main = Node.printLines [ same Empty (Holds (Payload 1)), String.fromInt 0 ]
